<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Like Games! – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> I Like School!</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> I Like Games!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: text: Identify objects on the pages of picture books, using simple words.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can identify familiar objects in picture books by saying simple words to a peer (e.g., 'game' or 'pencil') when asked or when another student shows interest. Can use gestures or point to objects in picture books to help peers understand preferences (e.g., pointing to a book and saying 'book' to support a peer's understanding). Can help a peer understand preferences by connecting words to objects in picture books (e.g., saying 'I like books.' while pointing to a book or 'This is math.' when pointing to numbers).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; text-align: left; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; text-align: left; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; text-align: left; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and initiates a quick "What do you like at school?" review. The teacher shows flashcards of school activities (e.g., 'art', 'music', 'math', 'playground', 'English', 'games', 'teacher'). For each flashcard, the teacher asks, "Do you like [art]?" and encourages students to respond with "Yes, I do!" or "No, I don't!" using TPR (e.g., thumbs up/down).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher shows a simple, pre-made "My Favorite Things Collage" (e.g., a few cut-out pictures glued to a paper). The teacher points to each picture and models simple sentences while using gestures: "Look! This is math. I like math!" (pointing to numbers). "This is games. I like games!" (pointing to a board game). The teacher emphasizes the /l/ sound in 'like' and the /g/ sound in 'games', and the sounds of letters in 'math' and 'games', encouraging choral repetition of "I like..." and the object names.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Am I showing *my* favorite things or *your* favorite things?" (Expected answer: "Your favorite things!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Am I using pictures and words to tell you?" (Expected answer: "Yes!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "I Like School!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, colorful picture book about school activities or a teacher's more elaborate "My Favorite Things Collage" that includes words and pictures. (Genre: Picture book/visual display; Register: Simple and informal; Format: Illustrated pages/collage)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students can identify familiar objects in picture books by saying simple words to a peer.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to various objects or activities in the picture book/collage. "What is this?" (e.g., "Art!" or "Playground!"). Students respond chorally or individually.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to a picture and expresses a preference: "I like music." Then asks, "Do I like music?" Students respond with "Yes!" or "No!" based on the teacher's statement.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher holds up two flashcards (e.g., 'math' and 'English') and says a target word (e.g., 'math'). Students point to the correct flashcard.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Subject + verb ('I like school.'), Present simple ('I like games.'), Yes/No questions ('Do you like it?' 'Yes.').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> teacher, art, playground, science, music, math, English, enjoy, choose, create, count, build, sing, share, look, write, color, interesting, funny, boring, exciting, happy, sad, favorite, colorful.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> /l/ in 'like', /g/ in 'games'; phonemic awareness for sounds of letters in 'math' and 'games'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Expressing likes and dislikes ('I like...', 'I don't like...'); Answering preference questions ('Yes' or 'No').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sociolinguistic:</b> Sharing preferences and understanding a peer's preferences about school and activities.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: "Point and Say"</b>
                        Students work in pairs. The teacher displays large flashcards or pictures related to the school theme (e.g., 'math', 'games', 'art', 'music'). Students take turns pointing to a picture and saying "This is [object]." (e.g., "This is math."). The teacher circulates, providing feedback on pronunciation, especially /l/ and /g/, and encouraging clear articulation of 'math' and 'games'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: "My Favorite Word"</b>
                        Students remain in pairs. Each pair is given a small set of picture cards (or uses pictures from a book). Student A points to a picture (e.g., 'playground') and asks, "What is this?" Student B responds, "Playground!" Then Student A says, "I like playground!" The roles are then reversed. The teacher reminds students to use gestures and clear words to help their partner understand.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction: "Help Your Friend Understand"</b>
                        Students are seated in pairs, each with either their incomplete collage or a picture book. Student A chooses a picture (e.g., 'music') and 'mediates' its meaning and their preference to Student B. Student A points to the picture and says, "This is music. I like music!" (using gestures). Student B then confirms understanding, perhaps by saying, "Oh, music!" or pointing to the same picture. The teacher encourages students to ask 'Do you like it?' to their partner and respond with 'Yes' or 'No' while pointing.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "I Like School!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Guided presentation and peer explanation of their "My Favorite Things Collage." (21st Century Skills Project Focus)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work individually or in small groups (if they need assistance) to finalize their "My Favorite Things Collage." They glue pre-cut pictures (e.g., 'art', 'math', 'games', 'music', 'playground', 'teacher') onto a piece of paper. The teacher provides support and reminds students of the vocabulary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Once collages are ready (even if simple, with just a few pictures), students sit in small groups of 3-4 peers.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each student takes a turn *mediating* their collage to their group. They hold up their collage, point to a picture, and clearly state: "This is [object]. I like [object]!" (e.g., "This is math. I like math!" or "Look! Games! I like games!"). They use gestures and simple words to help their peers understand the content and their preferences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Peers are encouraged to ask simple questions like "What is this?" (pointing to an item on the collage) or "Do you like it?" The presenting student attempts to respond using 'yes' or 'no' and pointing, effectively mediating the information.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in using simple phrases for expressing preferences and identifying objects, collaborative interaction through asking and answering questions, and successfully completing the communicative task of explaining their collage and helping peers understand its content.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist during group presentations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will present their "My Favorite Things Collage" to a small group of peers. They will use simple words and gestures to identify and express preferences for at least 2-3 items on their collage (e.g., "This is art. I like art!"). They will also attempt to respond to a peer's simple question (e.g., "Do you like games?") with 'Yes' or 'No', and point to clarify.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Students pronounce target words like 'like', 'games', and 'math' clearly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Correctly identifies and names 2-3 collage items (e.g., 'art', 'math', 'games', 'music').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Mediation:</b> Uses simple phrases ('I like...', 'This is...') and gestures/pointing to effectively help peers understand the content of their collage.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Attempts to listen and respond to peer questions or shows interest in a peer's collage.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students will complete a quick reflection by showing 'thumbs up' or 'thumbs down' or coloring a happy/sad face:</p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I show my friends my favorite things on my collage?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I say 'I like...' for my favorite things?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite part of making and sharing your 'My Favorite Things Collage'?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy or difficult about telling your friends about your collage and helping them understand?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students effectively use simple words and gestures to help their peers understand their collage? Were the target phrases ('I like...', 'This is...') used appropriately for mediation?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments could be made to better support Kinder students in mediating information, especially for those who struggled with verbal output? Perhaps more pre-made sentence strips or peer modeling.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can I build upon this foundational mediation skill in future themes, encouraging students to explain and interpret simple information for others in creative ways?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson served as a culmination, consolidating vocabulary and communication skills through the "My Favorite Things Collage" project. The next unit will introduce a new theme, building upon these foundational communicative and mediation skills, encouraging students to continue expressing and clarifying their ideas for peers.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>